# wmt_drv.ko creates /dev/wmtdetect and /dev/stpwmt, which wmt_loader opens.
#
# `on boot`, NOT `on post-fs` -- and this is a REVERT, deliberately, with the
# measurement that motivated the move still standing.
#
# The move to `on post-fs` was correct on its own terms: wmt_loader is
# class early_hal, started from /init.rc's `on late-fs`, so loading the module
# at `on boot` puts it AFTER its only consumer, and the boot capture shows
# wmt_loader spinning 926 ms in the retry loop inside the blob
# ("chipId detect failed, retrying, left retryCounter:%d"). post-fs runs about
# 23 ms before class_start early_hal and needs nothing the module does not have.
#
# It also broke Wi-Fi, and the reason is worth reading before anyone tries again
# (E-045). wlan_assistant reads the WIFI record out of
# /mnt/vendor/nvdata/APCFG/APRDEB and hands the MAC and the regulatory data to
# the WLAN driver. Bringing the connectivity driver up ~1 s earlier made
# vendor.connsys.driver.ready fire before NVRAM was restored; wlan_assistant
# found nothing and exited in 17 ms, and the driver took a RANDOM MAC with no
# country data -- which costs the entire 5 GHz band and makes the
# NVRAM-derived string field read "NVRAM WARNING: Err = 0x01".
#
# ANDing vendor.service.nvram_init=Ready onto the trigger was tried and is NOT
# sufficient: measured on the handset, nvram_daemon publishes that property
# 17.7 SECONDS before it finishes and exits, and wlan_assistant started at
# nvram_init=Ready still exits in 17 ms. The binary's own readiness property is
# `vendor.mtk.nvram.ready`, which is EMPTY on this device and therefore never a
# usable gate either.
#
# So the working signal is nvram_daemon actually exiting, and the only thing
# that has been PROVEN to work on this handset is the original `on boot`
# ordering plus a manual start afterwards. Reverted to the proven one.
#
# TO TRY AGAIN: put the insmod back on post-fs and gate the start on
# `init.svc.nvram_daemon=stopped` instead, then verify
# `cat /sys/class/net/wlan0/address` is 2c:45:67:89:f9:2c and
# `dmesg | grep fgNvramAvailable` reads 1 -- on a fresh flash, because a wiped
# /data changes how long nvram_daemon takes.
on boot
    exec u:r:vendor_modprobe:s0 -- /vendor/bin/insmod /vendor/lib/modules/wmt_drv.ko

on property:vendor.connsys.driver.ready=yes
    exec u:r:vendor_modprobe:s0 -- /vendor/bin/insmod /vendor/lib/modules/wmt_chrdev_wifi.ko
    exec u:r:vendor_modprobe:s0 -- /vendor/bin/insmod /vendor/lib/modules/wlan_drv_${ro.vendor.wlan.gen}.ko
    exec u:r:vendor_modprobe:s0 -- /vendor/bin/insmod /vendor/lib/modules/bt_drv.ko
    exec u:r:vendor_modprobe:s0 -- /vendor/bin/insmod /vendor/lib/modules/gps_drv.ko

# wlan_assistant needs BOTH the connectivity driver AND NVRAM. It reads the
# WIFI record out of /mnt/vendor/nvdata/APCFG/APRDEB and hands the MAC and the
# regulatory/calibration data to the WLAN driver; started before nvram_daemon
# has published, it finds nothing and exits, and the driver falls back to a
# RANDOM MAC with no country data -- which costs the 5 GHz band entirely and
# makes the device's own SSID field read "NVRAM WARNING: Err = 0x01".
#
# That dependency was never expressed. It held by accident while wmt_drv.ko was
# insmod'ed at `on boot`, because the extra second was enough for nvram_daemon
# to win the race. Moving the insmod to `on post-fs` -- correct in itself, it
# put the module before its consumer instead of 926 ms after -- made
# connsys.driver.ready fire earlier and the race started going the other way.
# Stock has the same unexpressed dependency and the same accidental ordering.
#
# Proven on the handset: with the driver up but wlan_assistant dead,
# `glLoadNvram: fgNvramAvailable = 0` and wlan0 is 00:08:22:e0:b7:fe; a manual
# `start wlan_assistant` afterwards gives `fgNvramAvailable = 1` and
# 2c:45:67:89:f9:2c, the address the factory wrote into NVRAM.
#
# Two property triggers ANDed: system/core/init/action_parser.cpp accepts `&&`
# between property triggers and fires the action when both hold.
# The nvram_init=Ready half is kept because the dependency is REAL and should
# be stated even though it is not sufficient on its own -- see the header.
on property:vendor.connsys.driver.ready=yes && property:vendor.service.nvram_init=Ready
    start wlan_assistant
